# ai.wing — build changelog & architectural decisions

Running log of every significant structural change, feature addition and
architectural decision made on this project. Entries are append-only and
newest-last, so a change can be traced and reverted without guesswork.

---

## [0.1.0] — Initial build: altafiber Authorized Retailer landing site

### Stack
- **Next.js 16** (App Router) + **React 19** + **TypeScript 7**
- **Tailwind CSS v4** — tokens declared with `@theme` in `app/globals.css`;
  there is deliberately no `tailwind.config.js`, v4 reads the CSS.
- **lenis** — smooth scrolling, and the only animation dependency. Everything
  else in the motion layer is hand-rolled (see *Animation architecture*).

### Decision: centralized data architecture
`lib/content.ts` is the single source of truth. No TSX file contains a
hard-coded price, speed, fee or term. Changing a number there cascades to:
- the hero price anchor
- every plan card in every service line
- the fine-print comparison grid
- the FAQ answers
- the footer legal block and the page metadata

Implemented via:
- `PlanItem` interface exactly as specified in the brief, plus three optional
  presentation-only extras (`badge`, `tagline`, `unit`).
- Selectors — `plansByLine()`, `getPlan()`, `leadPlan`, `startingPrice`,
  `slowestMbps`, `fastestMbps`, `ctaLabel()`, `speedLabel()`. Layout files call
  these, never the raw `plans` array.
- `<PriceLockup plan={plan} />` is the only price renderer on the site. Visual
  flex row: `$` symbol, dominant integer (2.5rem / 3rem / 3.5rem by size prop),
  muted cents + `/mo`. Unpriced plans render "Custom" plus their qualifier
  rather than an empty lockup.

### Decision: data-driven section visibility
`activeServiceSections` filters `serviceSections` down to lines that actually
have plans. Cable and Mobile are declared in canonical order but hold zero
plans, so they never render — no empty shells, no "call to ask" placeholders.
`app/page.tsx` maps over the filtered list, so adding a Cable plan to
`lib/content.ts` would make a Cable section appear in the correct slot with no
layout edit.

### Decision: CTA label rule lives in data, not markup
`ctaLabel(plan)` returns `"Call to order"` when `plan.price` is a number and
`"Call for pricing"` when it is not. Defined once so the two can never drift
apart across sections. The header and footer are the only two places the raw
phone number is the visible button text.

### Decision: every `tel:` link renders through `<CallButton />`
That component is what stamps `data-call-cta` (plus a `data-call-context` for
analytics) onto the anchor, which makes the attribute structurally guaranteed
rather than something to remember per usage. The one inline `tel:` link in the
service-section footnote carries the attribute explicitly.

### Brand system
Colors were lifted from altafiber.com's compiled stylesheet (`dist/master.css`)
where they ship as named Tailwind utilities, so these are the literal brand
hexes rather than eyeballed approximations:

| Token | Hex | altafiber class |
|---|---|---|
| `navy` | `#012639` | `.bg-navy-blue` |
| `navy-reduced` | `#394758` | `.bg-navy-blue-reduced` |
| `navy-light` | `#82939b` | `.bg-navy-blue-light` |
| `dark-blue` | `#005bb5` | `.bg-dark-blue` |
| `fiber-blue` | `#4294f7` | `.text-blue` |
| `fiber-tint` | `#edf5ff` | `.bg-lightest-fiber-blue` |
| `alta-green` | `#3dc37f` | `.bg-emerald` |
| `alta-teal` | `#22d3c5` | `.bg-enterprise-green` |
| `charcoal` | `#515156` | `.bg-charcoal-gray` |
| `velvet` | `#101014` | `.bg-velvet-black` |
| `light-gray` | `#ebebed` | `.bg-light-gray` |
| `lightest-gray` | `#f6f6fa` | `.bg-lightest-gray` |
| `off-white` | `#f6f6f6` | `.bg-off-white` |

**Typography:** altafiber ships Gilroy, which is proprietary. Substituted
**Figtree** via `next/font/google` — same geometric-humanist character, one
self-hosted variable file, `display: swap`, so there is no FOUT and no layout
shift.

### Animation architecture
Deliberately hand-rolled on IntersectionObserver + rAF + CSS transitions rather
than a scroll library, because the brief asks for scroll-heavy motion *and*
PageSpeed. What that buys: transitions live in CSS (compositor-only —
`transform`, `opacity`, `clip-path`), observers disconnect after firing, and no
rAF loop runs for an element that is off screen.

Implemented in `lib/hooks.ts` and `components/fx/`:

| Requirement | Implementation |
|---|---|
| Lenis smooth scrolling | `components/providers/SmoothScroll.tsx` — also intercepts `a[href^="#"]` so anchor nav eases |
| Black hole / fiber-optic background | `fx/FiberField.tsx` — canvas, particles spiral into an event horizon with accretion rings |
| Scroll-scrubbed assembly | `fx/GatewayAssembly.tsx` — home-network diagram builds in six scrubbed stages, reverses on scroll up |
| Scroll-triggered text reveals | `ui/RevealText.tsx` — per-line masked slide-up |
| Parallax depth | `fx/Parallax.tsx` — transform-only, sampled by `useScrollProgress` |
| SVG path draw | `fx/FiberPaths.tsx` — `stroke-dashoffset` draw plus a travelling light pulse |
| Magnetic buttons | `useMagnetic()` → `ui/MagneticButton.tsx` |
| Clip-path wipes | `.wipe` utility, used by the fine-print table, FAQ and closing CTA |
| 3D card tilt | `useTilt()` in `ui/PlanCard.tsx`, with a cursor-tracking sheen on the featured card |
| Staggered grid entrance | `--reveal-delay` per index in the service and feature grids |
| Infinite marquee | `ui/Marquee.tsx` — duplicated track translating `-50%`, pure CSS |

**Guards on every effect:** `prefers-reduced-motion` collapses the whole system
to a static page (global override at the end of `globals.css`); pointer effects
short-circuit on `(hover: none)`; the canvas caps DPR at 1.5, scales particle
count to area, and stops its loop when off screen *or* when the tab is hidden.

### Verified content
Every claim on the page was checked against altafiber.com before it was written.
Plans, rates, terms and fees come from `/plans-and-pricing`, `/tv`,
`/home-phone`, `/internet` and `/internet/fioptics-home-bundle`; the data policy
comes from altafiber's help-center answer on data caps.

Service lines confirmed: **Fiber, Bundles, TV, Phone**.
Confirmed *not* offered, therefore omitted entirely: **Cable** (altafiber is
100% fiber) and **Mobile** (absent from altafiber's product navigation).

### Negative constraints honoured
- No support-routing copy anywhere — the phrase and the concept are absent.
- Hero keeps **both** the ZIP checker and a call button.
- Plan/section CTAs read "Call to order" / "Call for pricing"; the raw number
  appears as button text only in the header and footer.
- No competitor bashing and no self-deprecating "we are only a channel" copy.
- No empty sections and no "call to ask" placeholder cards.
- No Broadband Facts label link.
- No "Pricing observed [Date]" stamps — there are no date strings on the page.
- No "Is this the official altafiber site?" FAQ.

### Trust chips — deviation from the brief, and why
The brief proposed `No Credit Checks`, `No Data Caps` and
`Free eero Router Included` *(if verified)*. Verification result:

- **No Data Caps** — verified. altafiber's help centre states it does not cap,
  limit or throttle residential data. **Kept.**
- **Free eero Router Included** — **contradicted** by altafiber's own pricing:
  the 100 Mbps – 2 Gig tiers carry a $15/mo gateway rate, and Fioptics Home is
  $20/mo except on 3 Gig. **Dropped** — it would be a false advertised
  inclusion and a Google Ads misrepresentation risk.
- **No Credit Checks** — no supporting statement found anywhere on
  altafiber.com. **Dropped** as unverifiable.

Replaced with claims that are each traceable to altafiber.com: `Symmetrical
Speeds`, `99.99% Reliable`, `Free Activation`, `30-Day Money-Back Guarantee`.
The real equipment costs are disclosed in full in the hardware grid instead.

### Layout notes
- Footer follows the `bestfiberinternet.us` pattern: brand block + link columns
  on the top row, a dedicated "Offer details & required disclosures" band, then
  a bottom bar with policy links left and copyright + reseller line right.
- Disclosure bar is `sticky top-0`, header is `sticky top-9` beneath it, so the
  retailer disclosure is on screen for the entire scroll and has no dismiss
  control.
- `overflow-x: clip` on `body`, `min-width: 0` on all elements and a `.shell`
  wrapper with a 16px gutter guarantee no horizontal overflow at 320px.

