## [0.7.0] — Photography

Seven generated images supplied. They arrived as 2.3 MB PNGs (16 MB total) at
1408×768, not the aspect ratios in the brief, and each carried Gemini's sparkle
watermark in the bottom-right corner.

### Preprocessing
A one-off sharp pass: trim 13% off the right edge (clears the watermark on all
seven without touching any subject, since every composition sits centre or
centre-left), resize, and convert to WebP at q82. Blur placeholders were
generated at the same time into `public/images/manifest.json`.

**16 MB → 821 KB.** The PNG originals were moved to `design-source/`, outside
`public/`, so they never deploy.

| File | Output | Size |
|---|---|---|
| `hero-home.webp` | 1200×752 | 85 KB |
| `install-technician.webp` | 1200×752 | 139 KB |
| `why-fiber-bg.webp` | 1400×878 | 151 KB |
| `tv-living-room.webp` | 1200×752 | 95 KB |
| `wifi-gateway.webp` | 1000×627 | 47 KB |
| `cta-home-evening.webp` | 1400×898 | 171 KB |
| `og-cover.jpg` | 1200×630 | 133 KB |

### Placement
`lib/images.ts` is the registry — intrinsic sizes and blur placeholders come
from the manifest, alt text is written alongside the rest of the site copy.

- **Hero** — bleeds off the right edge with a white fade into the copy, strand
  motif riding over it, `priority` since it is the LCP element. Below `lg` it
  runs as a band beneath the availability check rather than being dropped.
- **How it works** — installation photograph beneath the three steps.
- **Why 100% fiber** — dusk street behind the scrim.
- **Fioptics+ TV** — living-room band above the plan cards.
- **Equipment and fees** — router photograph beside the section heading.
- **Closing CTA** — house at dusk behind the scrim.
- **og:image + twitter:card** — wired into the layout metadata.

The strand motif over the hero was dropped from opacity 1.0 / 34 strands to
0.3 / 22, because at full weight over a photograph it read as moiré.

### The contrast audit was wrong, twice, and it mattered

Adding white text over photographs is exactly where the previous audit broke
down, and it had been quietly wrong for several passes:

1. **It substituted a hardcoded proxy for gradients.** Any `linear-gradient`
   backdrop was scored against `#0a3a72` instead of the real colour. That hid a
   genuine failure: the "Why fiber" and closing CTA gradients ended at
   `#2f86f0`, which gives white text **3.63:1** — under the 4.5 AA floor. Both
   gradients were re-cut so every stop clears 6:1.
2. **It only walked ancestors.** Once the scrim became a *sibling* overlay above
   an image, the walker found no opaque background and fell back to white,
   scoring white-on-white at 1.0 and reporting it as a failure — noise that
   would have masked anything real.

Rewrote the resolver to composite what is actually painted: it walks ancestors,
picks up absolutely-positioned sibling overlays, and for `<img>` siblings
samples the real pixel under the text via canvas, accounting for `object-fit:
cover` and `object-position`.

With a trustworthy measurement it immediately found **three real failures** —
the feature cards on the blue band used `bg-white/[0.08]`, tinting the backdrop
*lighter* under white text (3.57, 3.79, 4.20). Cards now tint darker
(`bg-navy/45`) and the body text went to `text-white/90`.

The probe is saved for reuse in the session scratchpad as `contrast-probe.js`.

### Verified
| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `next build` | passes |
| `tsc --noEmit` | clean |
| Images shipped | 7, **821 KB total** (from 16 MB) |
| Images loading | all 7 (the 8th `<img>` node is the `lg:hidden` mobile hero, correctly not loaded on desktop) |
| Missing `alt` | 0 — decorative backgrounds carry `alt=""` + `aria-hidden` |
| Contrast, `/` (pixel-sampling audit) | 386 nodes, **0 failures**; lowest 3.08:1 is the 80px H1 accent, which needs 3.0 |
| Scrim worst case (white pixel through lowest alpha) | 5.03:1 |
| Horizontal overflow at 320px | none |

