## [0.8.0] — Hero rebuilt on the photograph; photos as section backgrounds

### Hero
The panel-and-fade treatment was replaced. The photograph is now the section
background, edge to edge and flush against the header, with a white gradient
across its left half so the copy sits on clean white. No panel seam, and the
strand motif was removed from the hero entirely.

The gradient ramp is long (solid to 42%, then easing out to 88%) rather than
short. A short ramp leaves a visible vertical seam where white meets photo; it
still holds >=0.92 opacity out to 54%, past where the type ends.

**On the reported gap between navbar and hero:** measured `heroTop === headerBottom
=== 109` — there was never a gap in the layout. What read as one was the hero's
own top padding on the white side. The full-bleed photograph now runs to the
header edge, so it is unambiguous.

### Photographs as section backgrounds
- **How it works** — the technician photo is the whole section background under
  a white scrim (0.93 → 0.84 top to bottom). The section keeps dark text, so
  the scrim is white rather than dark. The standalone banner was removed as
  redundant, and the step markers' `ring-surface-alt` became `ring-white/85`
  since that background colour no longer exists here.
- **Fioptics+ TV** — the living-room photo is the section background under a
  deliberately thin dark scrim (0.54–0.66). That picture is a dark evening
  room, so the section had to flip to light-on-dark: `SectionHead` inverts, the
  footnote and its inline link invert, and the plan cards stay white.
- **Closing CTA** — blue overlay reduced from 0.90–0.93 to 0.76–0.82 so the
  house reads through it.

### The contrast probe was wrong three more times

Every one of these was the measuring tool, not the site — worth recording,
because each failure mode looked exactly like a real defect:

1. **Composited every gradient stop** instead of evaluating the gradient at the
   element's position. Fixed by parsing the stops and interpolating at the
   element's own x (or y, by gradient direction).
2. **Prepended overlay siblings one at a time**, which reversed their paint
   order — a scrim ended up painted *under* the image it was supposed to cover.
3. **Never descended into nested overlays.** The hero's white wash is a child of
   the image wrapper, not a sibling of the text, so the probe sampled the dark
   photo and never applied the white on top — reporting the hero headline at
   1.46:1 when it is fine.

The rewritten resolver walks the ancestor chain top-down, collects each
covering sibling's subtree in DOM (= paint) order, evaluates positional
gradients at the element's coordinates, and samples real image pixels through
`object-fit`/`object-position`. Under it: **386 nodes, 0 failures.**

The lesson worth keeping: a contrast audit that cannot see what is actually
painted will report confident nonsense in both directions — it hid a genuine
3.63:1 gradient failure earlier, and here invented eight that did not exist.

### Verified
| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `next build` | passes |
| `tsc --noEmit` | clean |
| Gap between header and hero | none — `heroTop === headerBottom` |
| Strand motif in hero | removed |
| Contrast, `/` (paint-order probe) | 386 nodes, **0 failures**; lowest 3.08:1 is the 68px H1 accent, which needs 3.0 |

